 Modes are `pr=null` (open the PR, `NEXT: await-merge`, unless the "Merged-unclosed" lookup finds one) and `pr.state="open"` (merge it). DoD checks gate publication but are not user approvals. The main orchestrator performs the merge itself where the Git host allows it — who merges, and the fact that merging is never closure, is stated once in `git-strategy.md` "Merging a PR". Merge mode confirms the sprint PR merged, writes nothing on `git.base_branch`, and returns `NEXT: await-closure`; the sprint stays at its active path with `phase="pr"`, `pr.state="open"`. One PR per sprint.
 
-**Merged-unclosed**: a sprint folder at the active path with `phase="pr"` and a `pr.number` that `gh pr view <n> --json state,mergeCommit` reports `MERGED`. The base copy carries `pr.number` because `pr` open mode commits and pushes its `state.json.pr` write before `NEXT: await-merge`. A PR merged before that write reached the sprint branch (by hand, or after a failed push) leaves base at `pr=null`, so a `phase="pr"` sprint without `pr.number` is looked up by head branch, `gh pr list --head <state.branch> --state all --json number,state,mergeCommit`: a `MERGED` hit is merged-unclosed with that number, which the closure write records; an `OPEN` hit resumes merge mode with that number; no hit means open mode. `asd-sprint` detects it on `NEXT: await-closure` and on any later invocation, and before starting or resuming anything else requests the hard sprint-closure approval (`checkpoints.md` "Gate policy") with the completion evidence. Refusal leaves the sprint active and resumable. Approval is carried into the new-sprint flow, and the one-active-sprint rule exempts that sprint within it. The session-start hook reports the state offline (`phase="pr"`, `pr.number` set, `state.branch` ≠ the current branch), display only, so it misses a sprint without `pr.number`; `asd-sprint` confirms through `gh` before asking.
